Subject: [PATCH 1/3] Quarantine a MIDI port whose close failed instead of freeing it

Closing a port whose device has dropped off the USB bus crashes the editor,
whether the close comes from Reconnect, entering play mode or a recompile.

On WinMM, RtMidi 6.0's MidiInWinMM::closePort deletes each sysex buffer and
only then checks the midiInUnprepareHeader result. On failure -- always the
case once the device is gone -- it calls midiInClose and returns early :
connected_ stays true, the critical section stays held, and the buffer list
is not cleared. Every later close walks the same list and deletes the same
buffers again. The destructor that Dispose reaches through rtmidi_in_free is
such a later close.

The previous fix ("close before free") relied on the first close leaving the
destructor nothing to do. That holds when the close succeeds, and it is only
needed when it does not. In this project it turned the std::terminate seen
before (RtMidi.dll, 0x40000015) into a heap corruption (ntdll, 0xc0000374),
25 s after the Roto-Control dropped off the bus :

    MidiInWinMM::openPort: error closing Windows MM MIDI input port
        (midiInUnprepareHeader).
    -- process gone, Application Error 1000, ntdll.dll, 0xc0000374

rtmidi_close_port catches that error and clears the wrapper's ok flag, so a
failed close is visible from C#. When it fails, the port is now quarantined:
its message callback is cancelled, the SafeHandle is marked invalid (neither
Dispose nor the finalizer will call rtmidi_in_free), and it is kept in a
static list. The native object leaks (a few KB per incident) ; the OS handle
does not, the error path having called midiInClose. A port whose open failed
is not connected, so it is freed directly without a close.

The wrapper's error message is deliberately not read : rtmidi_c stores
err.what() of an exception that no longer exists once the catch returns.

Package: com.blindspot.kontrols 0.5.1 (+ column-resize 01/02, close-port-before-free)
Unity 6000.3.10f1 - RtMidi: jp.keijiro.rtmidi 2.2.0 (RtMidi 6.0, MinGW build)
Rationale, crash timeline and event logs: see RAPPORT.md shipped alongside.
